le" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/state.json" "file_sha256" "79004fa3fa51cf2a2777d0ed4ba28f70a7776f2ba9a7a98f4f8794d869078062"} {"question" "q-135450" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7ef70fe7b4b12a5ec69f5ea1dc190f1f351f350f2f30415b1235ad1687b3058d"} {"question" "q-135450" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/state.json" "file_sha256" "79004fa3fa51cf2a2777d0ed4ba28f70a7776f2ba9a7a98f4f8794d869078062"} {"question" "q-135450" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}0.296 \\\\ 0.704\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}0.312 \\\\ 0.688\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}0.324 \\\\ 0.676\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}0.364 \\\\ 0.636\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}0.434 \\\\ 0.566\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/state.json" "file_sha256" "79004fa3fa51cf2a2777d0ed4ba28f70a7776f2ba9a7a98f4f8794d869078062"} {"question" "q-135450" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}0.324 \\\\ 0.676\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/state.json" "file_sha256" "79004fa3fa51cf2a2777d0ed4ba28f70a7776f2ba9a7a98f4f8794d869078062"} {"question" "q-35845" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A certain company produces two types of smartphones: Phone $A$ and Phone $B.$ The following table describes how the number of customers changes between both types of smartphones each year:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Phone $A$** | **Phone $B$** |\n| To: | **Phone $A$** | $0.6$ | $0.3$ |\n| **Phone $B$** | $0.4$ | $0.7$ |  |\n\nThat is, $40%$ of Phone $A$ customers switch to Phone $B$ each year, and $30%$ of Phone $B$ customers switch to Phone $A.$\n\nCurrently, $600\\,000$ customers use Phone $A,$ while $400\\,000$ customers use Phone $B.$ How many customers will use Phone $A$ in one year? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/state.json" "file_sha256" "79004fa3fa51cf2a2777d0ed4ba28f70a7776f2ba9a7a98f4f8794d869078062"} {"question" "q-35845" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the table, we can build the corresponding stochastic matrix: \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.6 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vector describing the number of customers for each smartphone is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}600\\,000 \\\\ 400\\,000\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDividing the vector above by the total number of customers $(1\\,000\\,000),$ we get the stochastic vector of the initial state:\n\n$$\n{x}_{0}=\\frac{1}{1\\,000\\,000}[\\begin{aligned}600\\,000 \\\\ 400\\,000\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.6 \\\\ 0.4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the state vector ${x}_{1}$ after one year, we apply one step of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.6 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.6 \\\\ 0.4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.48 \\\\ 0.52\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after one year, $48%$ of customers will use Phone $A.$ Computing $48%$ of $1\\,000\\,000,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n1\\,000\\,000\\cdot 0.48=480\\,000.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $480\\,000$ customers will use Phone $A$ in one year." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/state.json" "file_sha256" "79004fa3fa51cf2a2777d0ed4ba28f70a7776f2ba9a7a98f4f8794d869078062"} {"question" "q-35845" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7ef70fe7b4b12a5ec69f5ea1dc190f1f351f350f2f30415b1235ad1687b3058d"} {"question" "q-35845" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/state.json" "file_sha256" "79004fa3fa51cf2a2777d0ed4ba28f70a7776f2ba9a7a98f4f8794d869078062"} {"question" "q-35845" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "400\\,000"] ["math" "430\\,000"] ["math" "480\\,000"] ["math" "520\\,000"] ["math" "640\\,000"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/state.json" "file_sha256" "79004fa3fa51cf2a2777d0ed4ba28f70a7776f2ba9a7a98f4f8794d869078062"} {"question" "q-35845" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "480\\,000"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/state.json" "file_sha256" "79004fa3fa51cf2a2777d0ed4ba28f70a7776f2ba9a7a98f4f8794d869078062"} {"question" "q-135466" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A certain cinema sells three types of popcorn: traditional, butter, and caramelized. The following table describes how the number of customers changes between the three types of popcorn each month:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Traditional** | **Butter** | **Caramelized** |\n| To: | **Traditional** | $0.4$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ |\n| **Butter** | $0.3$ | $0.5$ | $0.2$ |  |\n| **Caramelized** | $0.3$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ |  |\n\nFor example, $30%$ of traditional popcorn customers switch to butter popcorn each month, and $40%$ of butter popcorn customers switch to caramelized popcorn.\n\nCurrently, $400$ customers buy traditional popcorn, $300$ customers buy butter popcorn, and $300$ customers buy caramelized popcorn. How many customers will purchase butter popcorn in one month? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/state.json" "file_sha256" "79004fa3fa51cf2a2777d0ed4ba28f70a7776f2ba9a7a98f4f8794d869078062"} {"question" "q-135466" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the table, we can build the corresponding stochastic matrix: \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.4 & 0.1 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.5 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.4 & 0.6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vector describing the number of customers for each type of popcorn customers is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}400 \\\\ 300 \\\\ 300\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDividing the vector above by the total number of customers $(1\\,000),$ we get the stochastic vector of the initial state:\n\n$$\n{x}_{0}=\\frac{1}{1\\,000}[\\begin{aligned}400 \\\\ 300 \\\\ 300\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.4 \\\\ 0.3 \\\\ 0.3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the state vector ${x}_{1}$ after one year, we apply one step of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.4 & 0.1 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.5 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.4 & 0.6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.4 \\\\ 0.3 \\\\ 0.3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.25 \\\\ 0.33 \\\\ 0.42\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after one year, $33%$ of customers will buy butter popcorn. Computing $33%$ of $1\\,000,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n1\\,000\\cdot 0.33=330.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $330$ customers will buy butter popcorn in one month." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/state.json" "file_sha256" "79004fa3fa51cf2a2777d0ed4ba28f70a7776f2ba9a7a98f4f8794d869078062"} {"question" "q-135466" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7ef70fe7b4b12a5ec69f5ea1dc190f1f351f350f2f30415b1235ad1687b3058d"} {"question" "q-135466" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/state.json" "file_sha256" "79004fa3fa51cf2a2777d0ed4ba28f70a7776f2ba9a7a98f4f8794d869078062"} {"question" "q-135466" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "290"] ["math" "310"] ["math" "330"] ["math" "360"] ["math" "420"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/state.json" "file_sha256" "79004fa3fa51cf2a2777d0ed4ba28f70a7776f2ba9a7a98f4f8794d869078062"} {"question" "q-135466" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "330"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/state.json" "file_sha256" "79004fa3fa51cf2a2777d0ed4ba28f70a7776f2ba9a7a98f4f8794d869078062"} {"question" "q-135465" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A certain company produces three types of ground coffee: fine, medium, and coarse. The following table describes how the number of customers changes between the three types of ground coffee each year:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Fine** | **Medium** | **Coarse** |\n| To: | **Fine** | $0.5$ | $0.2$ | $0.2$ |\n| **Medium** | $0.3$ | $0.6$ | $0.4$ |  |\n| **Coarse** | $0.2$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ |  |\n\nFor example, $30%$ of fine ground coffee customers switch to medium ground coffee each year, and $20%$ of coarse ground coffee customers switch to fine ground coffee.\n\nCurrently, $300\\,000$ customers buy fine ground coffee, $500\\,000$ customers buy medium ground coffee, and $200\\,000$ customers buy coarse ground coffee. How many customers will purchase fine ground coffee in one year? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/state.json" "file_sha256" "79004fa3fa51cf2a2777d0ed4ba28f70a7776f2ba9a7a98f4f8794d869078062"} {"question" "q-135465" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the table, we can build the corresponding stochastic matrix: \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.5 & 0.2 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.6 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.2 & 0.4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vector describing the number of customers for each type of ground coffee is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}300\\,000 \\\\ 500\\,000 \\\\ 200\\,000\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDividing the vector above by the total number of customers $(1\\,000\\,000),$ we get the stochastic vector of the initial state:\n\n$$\n{x}_{0}=\\frac{1}{1\\,000\\,000}[\\begin{aligned}300\\,000 \\\\ 500\\,000 \\\\ 200\\,000\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.3 \\\\ 0.5 \\\\ 0.2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the state vector ${x}_{1}$ after one year, we apply one step of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.5 & 0.2 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.6 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.2 & 0.4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.3 \\\\ 0.5 \\\\ 0.2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.29 \\\\ 0.47 \\\\ 0.24\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after one year, $29%$ of customers will buy fine ground coffee. Computing $29%$ of $1\\,000\\,000,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n1\\,000\\,000\\cdot 0.29=290\\,000.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $290\\,000$ customers will buy fine ground coffee in one year." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/state.json" "file_sha256" "79004fa3fa51cf2a2777d0ed4ba28f70a7776f2ba9a7a98f4f8794d869078062"} {"question" "q-135465" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7ef70fe7b4b12a5ec69f5ea1dc190f1f351f350f2f30415b1235ad1687b3058d"} {"question" "q-135465" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/state.json" "file_sha256" "79004fa3fa51cf2a2777d0ed4ba28f70a7776f2ba9a7a98f4f8794d869078062"} {"question" "q-135465" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "240\\,000"] ["math" "290\\,000"] ["math" "340\\,000"] ["math" "390\\,000"] ["math" "470\\,000"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/state.json" "file_sha256" "79004fa3fa51cf2a2777d0ed4ba28f70a7776f2ba9a7a98f4f8794d869078062"} {"question" "q-135465" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "290\\,000"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/state.json" "file_sha256" "79004fa3fa51cf2a2777d0ed4ba28f70a7776f2ba9a7a98f4f8794d869078062"} {"question" "q-135460" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A certain company produces three types of milk: whole, skim, and lactose-free. The following table describes how the number of customers changes between the three types of milk each year:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Whole** | **Skim** | **Lactose-free** |\n| To: | **Whole** | $0.5$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ |\n| **Skim** | $0.3$ | $0.4$ | $0.4$ |  |\n| **Lactose-free** | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $0.4$ |  |\n\nFor example, $30%$ of whole milk customers switch to skim milk each year, and $20%$ of lactose-free milk customers switch to whole milk.\n\nCurrently, $400\\,000$ customers consume whole milk, $400\\,000$ customers consume skim milk, and $200\\,000$ customers consume lactose-free milk. How many customers will consume whole milk after one year? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/state.json" "file_sha256" "79004fa3fa51cf2a2777d0ed4ba28f70a7776f2ba9a7a98f4f8794d869078062"} {"question" "q-135460" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the table, we can build the corresponding stochastic matrix: \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.5 & 0.3 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.4 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.3 & 0.4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vector describing the number of customers for each type of milk is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}400\\,000 \\\\ 400\\,000 \\\\ 200\\,000\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDividing the vector above by the total number of customers $(1\\,000\\,000),$ we get the stochastic vector of the initial state:\n\n$$\n{x}_{0}=\\frac{1}{1\\,000\\,000}[\\begin{aligned}400\\,000 \\\\ 400\\,000 \\\\ 200\\,000\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.4 \\\\ 0.4 \\\\ 0.2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the state vector ${x}_{1}$ after one year, we apply one step of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.5 & 0.3 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.4 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.3 & 0.4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.4 \\\\ 0.4 \\\\ 0.2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.36 \\\\ 0.36 \\\\ 0.28\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after one year, $36%$ of customers will consume whole milk. Computing $36%$ of $1\\,000\\,000,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n1\\,000\\,000\\cdot 0.36=360\\,000.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $360\\,000$ customers will consume whole milk after one year." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/state.json" "file_sha256" "79004fa3fa51cf2a2777d0ed4ba28f70a7776f2ba9a7a98f4f8794d869078062"} {"question" "q-135460" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7ef70fe7b4b12a5ec69f5ea1dc190f1f351f350f2f30415b1235ad1687b3058d"} {"question" "q-135460" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/state.json" "file_sha256" "79004fa3fa51cf2a2777d0ed4ba28f70a7776f2ba9a7a98f4f8794d869078062"} {"question" "q-135460" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "280\\,000"] ["math" "320\\,000"] ["math" "360\\,000"] ["math" "380\\,000"] ["math" "430\\,000"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/state.json" "file_sha256" "79004fa3fa51cf2a2777d0ed4ba28f70a7776f2ba9a7a98f4f8794d869078062"} {"question" "q-135460" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "360\\,000"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/state.json" "file_sha256" "79004fa3fa51cf2a2777d0ed4ba28f70a7776f2ba9a7a98f4f8794d869078062"} {"question" "q-135458" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A certain company produces two types of motorcycles: classic and high-cylinder. The following table describes how the number of customers changes between both types of motorcycles each decade:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Classic** | **High-Cylinder** |\n| To: | **Classic** | $0.6$ | $0.2$ |\n| **High-Cylinder** | $0.4$ | $0.8$ |  |\n\nThat is, $40%$ of classic motorcycle customers switch to high-cylinder each decade, and $20%$ of high-cylinder motorcycle customers switch to Classic.\n\nCurrently, $7\\,000$ customers use classic motorcycles while $3\\,000$ customers use high-cylinder motorcycles. How many customers will use high-cylinder motorcycles in one decade? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/state.json" "file_sha256" "79004fa3fa51cf2a2777d0ed4ba28f70a7776f2ba9a7a98f4f8794d869078062"} {"question" "q-135458" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the table, we can build the corresponding stochastic matrix: \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.6 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.8\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vector describing the number of customers for each type of motorcycle is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}7\\,000 \\\\ 3\\,000\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDividing the vector above by the total number of customers $(10\\,000),$ we get the stochastic vector of the initial state:\n\n$$\n{x}_{0}=\\frac{1}{10\\,000}[\\begin{aligned}7\\,000 \\\\ 3\\,000\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.7 \\\\ 0.3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the state vector ${x}_{1}$ after one decade, we apply one step of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.6 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.7 \\\\ 0.3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.48 \\\\ 0.52\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after one decade, $52%$ of customers will use high-cylinder motorcycles. Computing $52%$ of $10\\,000,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n10\\,000\\cdot 0.52=5\\,200.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $5\\,200$ customers will use high-cylinder motorcycles in one decade." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/state.json" "file_sha256" "79004fa3fa51cf2a2777d0ed4ba28f70a7776f2ba9a7a98f4f8794d869078062"} {"question" "q-135458" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7ef70fe7b4b12a5ec69f5ea1dc190f1f351f350f2f30415b1235ad1687b3058d"} {"question" "q-135458" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/state.json" "file_sha256" "79004fa3fa51cf2a2777d0ed4ba28f70a7776f2ba9a7a98f4f8794d869078062"} {"question" "q-135458" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "4\\,800"] ["math" "5\\,200"] ["math" "5\\,600"] ["math" "6\\,100"] ["math" "6\\,400"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/state.json" "file_sha256" "79004fa3fa51cf2a2777d0ed4ba28f70a7776f2ba9a7a98f4f8794d869078062"} {"question" "q-135458" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5\\,200"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/state.json" "file_sha256" "79004fa3fa51cf2a2777d0ed4ba28f70a7776f2ba9a7a98f4f8794d869078062"} {"question" "e-9162" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are stochastic matrices?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/assets/70ee929c71d89a443d3ea0efc39ed225f344685059b86a04285a118a476da0f8.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/example-9162.json" "file_sha256" "d8ebbebbe49fa420866cfd9b7fe1547a0eb9cfa771c7453cef4c6ee10a5db4c9"} {"question" "e-9162" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that a stochastic vector is a vector with nonnegative entries that add up to $1.$ A matrix is stochastic if all its columns are stochastic vectors.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices in turn.\n\n- Matrix $\\bf{U}$ is *not* a stochastic matrix since its first column is not a stochastic vector (it contains negative entries): \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-0.2 & 0 \\\\ 1.2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Matrix $\\bf{V}$ is a stochastic matrix. Indeed, each column is a stochastic vector (the corresponding entries are nonnegative and add up to one): \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}0.35 & 0.95 \\\\ 0.65 & 0.05\\end{aligned}]\\,\\begin{aligned}0.35+0.65=1 \\\\ 0.95+0.05=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Matrix $\\bf{W}$ is *not* a stochastic matrix since its third column is not a stochastic vector (the corresponding entries don't add up to one): \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 0 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.5 & 0 & 0.7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$\\bf{V}$ only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/example-9162.json" "file_sha256" "d8ebbebbe49fa420866cfd9b7fe1547a0eb9cfa771c7453cef4c6ee10a5db4c9"} {"question" "e-5598" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "There are two groups of people in a particular city: those who use their own vehicles and those who use public transport. A study has shown that, each year\n\n- $35\\%$ of people who used their own vehicles swap to public transport, and\n\n- $25\\%$ of people who used public transport swap to their own vehicles.\n\nGive the stochastic matrix that describes how people change their means of transportation each year." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/example-5598.json" "file_sha256" "4754af7501397f1042cc676821af8e414b0beab3e1eee5d62b9b67c56b2b98c7"} {"question" "e-5598" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We are given the following situation for each year: \n\n- $35\\%$ of people who used their own vehicles swap to public transport. This implies that $65\\%$ of them remain using their own vehicles.\n\n- $25\\%$ of people who used public transport swap to their own vehicles. This implies that $75\\%$ of them remain using public transport.\n\nWriting this information in tabular form, we obtain the following:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Vehicles** | **Public Transport ** |\n| To: | **Vehicles** | $0.65$ | $0.25$ |\n| **Public Transport** | $0.35$ | $0.75$ |  |\n\nNote that we can write a corresponding stochastic matrix as follows:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/assets/b21fee1abe56dcfdc687be14278c62a20d201ff00e96f1e5918ce22d9dc8b03c.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/example-5598.json" "file_sha256" "4754af7501397f1042cc676821af8e414b0beab3e1eee5d62b9b67c56b2b98c7"} {"question" "e-5971" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/assets/165917feacd28344502759fc6e03f96c5b54d9530701916b746fc489374a6d0b.png) \n\nConsider a Markov chain with the stochastic matrix $P$ and the initial state vector $\\mathbf{x}_0$ given above. Calculate the state vector $\\mathbf{x}_2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/example-5971.json" "file_sha256" "6add0b2ee858d3a1dd6549089e552e02d7e175defe660c2da3da36448a5a50e0"} {"question" "e-5971" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the state vector $\\mathbf{x}_2,$ we apply two steps of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x_1}=P\\mathbf{x}_0, \\qquad \\mathbf{x}_2=P\\mathbf{x}_1\n$$\n\nAfter the first step, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.5 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.2 \\\\ 0.8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.18 \\\\ 0.82\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, after the second step, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =P{x}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.5 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.18 \\\\ 0.82\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.172 \\\\ 0.828\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/example-5971.json" "file_sha256" "6add0b2ee858d3a1dd6549089e552e02d7e175defe660c2da3da36448a5a50e0"} {"question" "e-5599" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A certain industry produces $2$ types of sugar, namely sugar $A$ and sugar $B$. The following table describes how the number of consumers changes between both types of sugar in a month:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Sugar $A$** | **Sugar $B$** |\n| To: | **Sugar $A$** | $0.3$ | $0.45$ |\n| **Sugar $B$** | $0.7$ | $0.55$ |  |\n\nThat is, $70\\%$ of sugar $A$ customers switch to sugar $B$ each month, and $45\\%$ of sugar $B$ customers switch to sugar $A.$\n\nCurrently, $600$ customers use sugar $A,$ while $400$ customers use sugar $B.$ How many customers will use sugar $A$ in one month? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/example-5599.json" "file_sha256" "57e8baaeaeab55e7533bf633be8d2b7cb66468e73232ead6ea32eddcf8164d12"} {"question" "e-5599" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the table, we can build the corresponding stochastic matrix: \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/assets/451b82e8cb00ede7b0ddf73f35c0555d0eeeeefc5beb5ce4fddd54f0df5f2577.png)\n\nThe vector describing the number of customers for each type of sugar is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/assets/56952aeed1d718033af8a3e6d09e8e269f1d70aa8dc31609b8289f025f2e6bd6.png)\nDividing the vector above by the total number of customers $(1\\,000),$ we get the stochastic vector of the initial state:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/assets/cae1bed6b8b01b3ff66c2e8bacf7a10f6c0ece53f3aeac0fb1a51e8fc3c07e8e.png)\n\nTo find the state vector $\\mathbf{x}_1$ after one month, we apply one step of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_1=P\\mathbf{x}_0\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.3 & 0.45 \\\\ 0.7 & 0.55\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.6 \\\\ 0.4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.36 \\\\ 0.64\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after one month, $\\color{blue}36\\%$ of customers will use sugar $A.$ Computing $36\\%$ of $1\\,000,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n1\\,000 \\cdot 0.36 = 360.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $360$ customers will use sugar $A$ in one month." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/example-5599.json" "file_sha256" "57e8baaeaeab55e7533bf633be8d2b7cb66468e73232ead6ea32eddcf8164d12"}] "usage" {"q-35846" [] "e-5598" [] "e-5599" [] "q-35845" [] "q-109881" [] "e-5971" [] "e-9162" []} "basis" 1926 "retractions" [[17592186124686 :question/problem "Which of the following are stochastic vectors?\n\n$u = \\begin{bmatrix}0.5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0.5\\end{bmatrix}, v = \\begin{bmatrix}-0.45 \\\\ 1.55\\end{bmatrix}, w = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186124841 :question/problem "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\n$P = \\begin{bmatrix}0.2 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.8 & 0.5\\end{bmatrix}, x_{0} = \\begin{bmatrix}0.7 \\\\ 0.3\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider a Markov chain with the stochastic matrix $P$ and the initial state vector $x_{0}$ given above. Calculate the state vector $x_{2}$."] [17592186124840 :question/problem "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nA certain company produces two types of smartphones: Phone $A$ and Phone $B$. The following table describes how the number of customers changes between both types of smartphones each year:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  |  | **Phone $A$** | **Phone $B$** |\n| To: | **Phone $A$** | $0.6$ | $0.3$ |\n| **Phone $B$** | $0.4$ | $0.7$ |  |\n\nThat is, $40%$ of Phone $A$ customers switch to Phone $B$ each year, and $30%$ of Phone $B$ customers switch to Phone $A$.\n\nCurrently, $600000$ customers use Phone $A$, while $400000$ customers use Phone $B$. How many customers will use Phone $A$ in one year? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change."] [17592186124343 :question/problem "Which of the following are stochastic matrices?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-0.2 & 0 \\\\ 1.2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186124343 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a stochastic vector is a vector with nonnegative entries that add up to $1$. A matrix is stochastic if all its columns are stochastic vectors.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices in turn.\n\n- Matrix $\\bf{U}$ is *not* a stochastic matrix since its first column is not a stochastic vector (it contains negative entries):\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-0.2 & 0 \\\\ 1.2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n- Matrix $\\bf{V}$ is a stochastic matrix. Indeed, each column is a stochastic vector (the corresponding entries are nonnegative and add up to one):\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0.35 & 0.95 \\\\ 0.65 & 0.05\\end{bmatrix}0.35 + 0.65 = 1; 0.95 + 0.05 = 1$\n- Matrix $\\bf{W}$ is *not* a stochastic matrix since its third column is not a stochastic vector (the corresponding entries don't add up to one):\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 0 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.5 & 0 & 0.7\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$\\bf{V}$ only.\""] [17592186124271 :question/problem "There are two groups of people in a particular city: those who use their own vehicles and those who use public transport. A study has shown that, each year\n\n- $35\\%$ of people who used their own vehicles swap to public transport, and\n- $25\\%$ of people who used public transport swap to their own vehicles.\n\nGive the stochastic matrix that describes how people change their means of transportation each year."] [17592186124271 :question/worked-solution "We are given the following situation for each year:\n\n- $35\\%$ of people who used their own vehicles swap to public transport. This implies that $65\\%$ of them remain using their own vehicles.\n- $25\\%$ of people who used public transport swap to their own vehicles. This implies that $75\\%$ of them remain using public transport.\n\nWriting this information in tabular form, we obtain the following:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  |  | **Vehicles** | **Public Transport** |\n| To: | **Vehicles** | $0.65$ | $0.25$ |\n| **Public Transport** | $0.35$ | $0.75$ |  |\n\nNote that we can write a corresponding stochastic matrix as follows:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0.65 & 0.25 \\\\ 0.35 & 0.75\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186124281 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}0.5 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.9\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider a Markov chain with the stochastic matrix $P$ and the initial state vector $\\mathbf{x}_0$ given above. Calculate the state vector $\\mathbf{x}_2$."] [17592186124281 :question/worked-solution "To find the state vector $\\mathbf{x}_2$, we apply two steps of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x_1}=P\\mathbf{x}_0, \\qquad \\mathbf{x}_2=P\\mathbf{x}_1\n$$\n\nAfter the first step, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= Px_{0} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0.5 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0.2 \\\\ 0.8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0.18 \\\\ 0.82\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, after the second step, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= Px_{1} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0.5 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0.18 \\\\ 0.82\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0.172 \\\\ 0.828\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186124272 :question/problem "A certain industry produces $2$ types of sugar, namely sugar $A$ and sugar $B$. The following table describes how the number of consumers changes between both types of sugar in a month:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  |  | **Sugar $A$** | **Sugar $B$** |\n| To: | **Sugar $A$** | $0.3$ | $0.45$ |\n| **Sugar $B$** | $0.7$ | $0.55$ |  |\n\nThat is, $70\\%$ of sugar $A$ customers switch to sugar $B$ each month, and $45\\%$ of sugar $B$ customers switch to sugar $A$.\n\nCurrently, $600$ customers use sugar $A$, while $400$ customers use sugar $B$. How many customers will use sugar $A$ in one month? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change."] [17592186124272 :question/worked-solution "From the table, we can build the corresponding stochastic matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.3 & 0.45 \\\\ 0.7 & 0.55\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vector describing the number of customers for each type of sugar is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}600 \\\\ 400\\end{bmatrix}$\nDividing the vector above by the total number of customers $(1\\,000)$, we get the stochastic vector of the initial state:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}600 \\\\ 400\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the state vector $\\mathbf{x}_1$ after one month, we apply one step of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_1=P\\mathbf{x}_0\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= Px_{0} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0.3 & 0.45 \\\\ 0.7 & 0.55\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0.6 \\\\ 0.4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0.36 \\\\ 0.64\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after one month, $\\color{blue}36\\%$ of customers will use sugar $A$. Computing $36\\%$ of $1\\,000$, we obtain\n\n$$\n1\\,000 \\cdot 0.36 = 360\n$$\n\nTherefore, $360$ customers will use sugar $A$ in one month."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186121136 17592186121137 17592186121138 17592186121139 17592186121140 17592186122063 17592186122064 17592186122065 17592186122066 17592186122067 17592186122069 17592186122070 17592186122071 17592186122072 17592186122073] "immutable_fields" [[17592186121135 :answer-field/correct] [17592186121135 :answer-field/id] [17592186121135 :answer-field/key] [17592186121135 :answer-field/type] [17592186122062 :answer-field/correct] [17592186122062 :answer-field/id] [17592186122062 :answer-field/key] [17592186122062 :answer-field/type] [17592186122068 :answer-field/correct] [17592186122068 :answer-field/id] [17592186122068 :answer-field/key] [17592186122068 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
